# Git conventions

## Branches

- Default branch: `main`. Personal work happens on feature branches named `<initials>/<short-slug>` or `feat/<slug>`, `fix/<slug>`, `chore/<slug>`.
- Rebase, don't merge, when integrating upstream changes onto a feature branch: `git pull --rebase --autostash`.
- Never force-push `main`/`master`. On feature branches, prefer `git push --force-with-lease` (alias `git please`).

## Commits

- Imperative, short subject line, no trailing period. Lower-case verb start is fine.
  - Good: `add chezmoi bootstrap script`, `fix prompt segment for non-git dirs`.
  - Bad: `Added some files.`, `WIP`, `update`.
- Body (optional) wraps at 72 columns and explains **why**, not what.
- No emojis in commit messages unless the repo already uses them.
- Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`) are fine when the repo already uses them; do NOT introduce them unilaterally.

## Hygiene

- Stage hunks deliberately. Use `git add -p` when changes are mixed.
- Don't commit secrets, large binaries, or generated files. Add them to `.gitignore` instead.
- `git status` should be clean before switching branches; stash with `git stash -u` if not.
- Use `git commit --fixup=<sha>` + `git rebase -i --autosquash` for in-review polish.

## Safety

- Do NOT amend or rewrite a commit that has been pushed to a shared branch unless explicitly requested.
- Do NOT skip hooks (`--no-verify`).
- Confirm with the user before destructive operations: `reset --hard`, `clean -fdx`, `branch -D`, force pushes.
